        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Kalinago</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Lesser Antilles: at Spanish contact the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a>, Dominica and the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a>, including Guadeloupe, and possibly Barbados; Grenada and Saint Vincent; today the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago-territory">Kalinago Territory</a>, 3,700 acres on the east coast of Dominica; a Carib community at Arima in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>; the related <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garifuna">Garifuna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Cayo pottery in the islands c. 1000–1500, reaching Grenada and Saint Vincent around 1250; the change from Igneri to Island Carib culture perhaps around 1450; Spanish contact from 1492; the massacre on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a>, 1626; the Treaty of Saint Charles, 1660; British annexation of Dominica and Saint Vincent by 1763; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garifuna">Garifuna</a> expelled in 1797; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago-territory">Carib Territory</a> granted in 1903; the name Kalinago official in Dominica in 2015.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe">dugout canoes</a> and voyages between the islands; fishing; raids, and resistance to Spanish, French and English colonisers; the “poison arrow curtain”; an Arawakan language with a men’s Cariban pidgin; ancestors’ bones kept in the house; Cayo and Suazan Troumassoid pottery; bush medicine</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">the accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> and the Spanish; missionaries such as Raymond Breton (in the islands 1635–1653), Cesar de Rochefort and Jean Baptiste Labat; archaeology of Cayo and Suazan Troumassoid pottery in Grenada and Saint Vincent, with radiocarbon dating; ancient DNA; the work of Irving Rouse, William F. Keegan and Corinne L. Hofman; Kalinago oral testimony and communities today.</span></div>
        </div>
