    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Kalinago</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>The Kalinago are an Indigenous people of the Lesser Antilles, islands in the Caribbean.</p><p>Kalinago is their own name, while Columbus and the Spanish called them Caribs.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalina-people.jpg" alt="Kalina people" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Kalina people <span class="kid-tale-cr">We El at Dutch Wikipedia, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Their old stories say their ancestors came from South America about 800 years ago.</p><p>Scholars now think these newcomers joined the peoples already living on the islands.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>The Kalinago are seafarers, and they travel and fish in dugout canoes made from trees.</p><p>Fish is still their main food, often cooked in one pot with yams and coconut cream.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>The Kalinago kept their freedom against Spain, but English and French settlers took their islands.</p><p>Almost 400 years ago, settlers killed about 2,000 Kalinago on the island of Saint Kitts.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garifuna.jpg" alt="Garifuna" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Garifuna <span class="kid-tale-cr">Agostino Brunias (ca. 1730 – 2 April 1796), Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>On Saint Vincent, Kalinago and free West Africans formed a new people, the Garifuna.</p><p>The British later forced the Garifuna to leave, and today they live mainly in Central America.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalinago-territory.jpg" alt="Kalinago Territory" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Kalinago Territory <span class="kid-tale-cr">Hans Hillewaert / CC BY-SA 4.0 / Biopics 8 February 2007, minor text change by Davidwr 29 July 2020, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Today most Kalinago live in the Kalinago Territory, on the island of Dominica.</p><p>They elect their own chief, and some still build canoes in the traditional way.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Long described as invaders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> who conquered the earlier Igneri, the Kalinago appear from archaeology and ancient DNA to have formed from the islands’ own peoples together with newcomers from the mainland.</li><li>They kept their independence in the Lesser Antilles against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>; Dominica and Saint Vincent were left to them as reserves in 1660, but the British had annexed both by 1763.</li><li>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> in 1626 English and French settlers massacred an estimated 2,000 Kalinago, and Spanish tales of Carib cannibalism, never backed by hard evidence, have been read as a campaign to justify such genocidal expeditions.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The English word “cannibal” comes from the Kalinago word karibna, “person”, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> spread stories of man-eating Caribs that he had apparently heard from their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> enemies.</p></aside>
    </section>

